<article class="env-corollary" id="A0DCF1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A0DCF1">Corollary <span data-tag="A0DCF1">7.2.2.30</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos. If $\calX $ has homotopy dimension $\leq n$, then $\calX $ has cohomological dimension $\leq n$. The converse holds provided that $\calX $ has finite homotopy dimension and $n \geq 2$. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Only the last claim requires proof. Suppose that $\calX $ has cohomological dimension $\leq n$ and homotopy dimension $\leq k$. We must show that every $n$-connective object $X$ of $\calX $ has a global section. Choose a truncation $X \rightarrow \tau _{\leq k-1} X$. Then $\tau _{\leq k-1} X$ is truncated and $n$-connective, so it admits a global section by Theorem <a href="/tag/519482" data-tag="519482">7.2.2.29</a>. Form a pullback square 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X' \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  X \ar [d] \\ 1 \ar [r] &amp;  \tau _{\leq k-1} X} \]
</div>
    <p>
       It now suffices to prove that $X'$ has a global section. But $X'$ is $k$-connective, and therefore has a global section in virtue of the assumption that $\calX $ has homotopy dimension $\leq k$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>